Rewrite be-cpp's regex code sets past ASCII, negated sets, and `.` into alternations std::regex can match, and throw when a pattern fails to compile.
Upstream: Temper branch fix-cpp-generics (a02a65a0), not yet merged.

std::regex works on bytes, so a set reaching past ASCII never matched
one UTF-8 code point; each becomes an ASCII bracket or UTF-8 byte
sequences, one code point each, and a compile failure throws
`std::runtime_error` naming the pattern rather than never matching.

